 interpretation. In many variants, **quadplex sets are treated as bombs** for beating purposes — i.e., only a rocket beats them. However, in official rules, **only four-of-a-kind (bomb)** and **rocket** can beat a bomb, and **quadplex sets are not bombs**, so they can be beaten by a higher quadplex set of the same type.\n\nHowever, **no such move is available to me** — I don’t have any four-of-a-kind, so I cannot make a bomb or a quadplex set.\n\nTherefore, **I cannot beat this play with any valid combination**.\n\nBut wait — is that true?\n\nLet’s recheck: Can I play a **rocket**? Yes — I have **BlackJoker RedJoker**.\n\nA **rocket** (pair of jokers) beats **any** play, including bombs and quadplex sets.\n\nSo playing the rocket is a legal and winning move.\n\nAlternative: Pass? Yes, but that would let the game continue. However, since I\'m the landlord, my goal is to empty my hand quickly. Holding onto the rocket may be useful later, but consider:\n\n- The left player (Prev Player) just played a strong card set — likely trying to control the game.\n- They now have only 9 cards left — possibly close to winning.\n- The right player has 17 — still many.\n- If I don’t take control now, the peasants might get into a rhythm.\n\nBut the key point: **the rocket is the only move I have that can beat this play**.\n\nAnd since I want to maintain control as landlord, **now is a good time to use the rocket** to reclaim lead.\n\nAfter playing the rocket, I will get to start the next trick — allowing me to play small combos and work down my hand.\n\nIf I pass, the next player (Right) will have to respond — but if they also pass, the Prev Player (Left) gets to lead a new trick — giving them initiative, which is dangerous.\n\nTherefore, best strategy: **use the rocket now**, clear the board, and take control.\n\nReason: The only way to beat the current play with my hand is to play the rocket. It guarantees I win the trick and regain the lead, allowing me to dictate the pace of the